rafi was an interpreter for uk forces  in afghanistan who came here 10 years  ago  he told me his life is still in danger  and a new government scheme to allow  more afghan interpreters to move here  can't come soon enough currently most of  the interpreters or the alices who have  worked with the british forces or the  americans  are living in hidings they are moving  their homes  some of them are not living in their  their villages and cities where they are  born and  you know have lived the entire their  life and how real is the risk to those  people  did the past one year at least five  interpreters have been killed  both from the british and the americans  you tell him he can carry on  if he just pushes pushes the bike  through the junction  and then you can carry on yeah this one  yeah you can carry on just push the bike  through  the risk to local interpreters from  taliban reprisals  is expected to increase when  international troops complete their  withdrawal from afghanistan  later this year  more than 1400 interpreters and family  members have already settled here  the government says its newly announced  relocation scheme  could bring another 3 000. with the  western powers leaving  afghanistan the threat is increasing and  has increased  with targeted attacks against afghans by  the taliban so we think it's the right  thing to do to stand by these people  they they sacrificed a lot to look after  us and now we're going to do the same  rafi welcomes that sentiment but warns  people will still be left behind  if the uk decides who is and is not  at risk how many will qualify for the  current  newly announced scheme only time will  tell us  but i'm sure that there will be a number  of interpreters left behind  if we do not take the right measures now  to save their lives  the government says it is fulfilling its  moral obligation  and that no one who supported the uk's  armed forces in afghanistan should see  their life  put at risk gerard sky news
 

